ignore/
promtail_logs.txt
loki_check.txt
promtail_check.txt

# Terraform (terraform/) — never commit state or credential env files
terraform/.terraform/
terraform/.terraform.lock.hcl.bak
terraform/terraform.tfstate
terraform/terraform.tfstate.backup
terraform/*.tfplan
terraform/secrets.enc.env.dec
terraform/*.auto.tfvars
# Plan files can contain resolved sensitive values (a saved plan embeds the computed `user_data`,
# which carries the Tailscale auth key while `enable_rebuild_server` is on). P3-04/P3-07 name them
# explicitly rather than relying on the one existing pattern.
*.plan
tfplan
plan.out
# Private key material must never be committed. On 2026-09-28 it turned out
# k8s/traefik/key.pem had been committed for four and a half months and was the key
# of the certificate the origin was serving; this belt-and-braces entry makes the
# mistake harder than gitleaks alone does.
#
# Two things are deliberately NOT affected:
#   - `secrets.sops.yaml` — SOPS ciphertext is the sanctioned way to keep a secret
#     in git. Note these patterns do not match it; do not add a `*.yaml` rule here.
#   - the patterns below are `.gitignore` rules, so they only affect files git does not already
#     track. (This bullet used to describe `archived/**`, a directory CLEAN-001 deleted on
#     2026-09-29 and which is not in the tree; its last line — "to files git already tracks" — was
#     a fragment left from an earlier edit, so the one statement worth keeping is restored here.)
# If a legitimate .pem/.key genuinely must be tracked, use `git add -f` and be ready
# to justify it — SOPS is almost certainly the right answer instead.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*

# Ansible's local collection/cache directory (never committed)
.ansible/
